From 32937adc8e611c1bf839b0905f71530a1d444284 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Oursland <jacob.oursland@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 01:12:05 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/18] solidcore-cadir: read cadmpeg's neutral CAD model
 (CADIR) straight into B-rep records
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A new solidcore crate reads the bodies of a CADIR model, the neutral model cadmpeg decodes
FreeCAD, Rhino and other CAD files into, into schema 4 records: each body region's faces, edges
and vertices go through the format-neutral assembly the STEP reader uses (pcurves, seams,
tolerances, the canonical order). SolidCraft reads FreeCAD and Rhino files through cadmpeg
today by having cadmpeg write STEP text and reading that back; this is the reader that
replaces the STEP round trip.

CADIR's topology conventions are STEP's (checked against cadmpeg's own STEP writer); its
curves keep their parameters, so an edge's interval carries over unchanged. Two places where
the STEP round trip lost information are read right: a sphere or torus with a negative
radius, whose surface normal points inward (the STEP writer drops the sign but not the side),
and a NURBS surface marked `normal_reversed`. A placed carrier is mapped exactly when its
placement is a rotation and uniform scale. Elliptical cones, parabolas, hyperbolas, composite
and polygonal carriers are refused with the reason, as are faces with no boundary.

Tested on cadmpeg's example cube (10 mm: 1000 mm³, faces outward), an edge whose vertices are
written the other way round, a face on a plane facing the other way, and hostile input (a
missing coedge, a vertex 5 mm off its edge). The crate joins the panic-site check; its one
site is a clamp between constants.

